The Nautilus DevOps team is currently engaged in a cleanup process, focusing on removing unnecessary data and services from their Azure environment. As part of the migration process, several resources were created for one-time use only, necessitating a cleanup effort to optimize their Azure environment.
A private blob container named xfusion-blob-9948 already exists in the westus region under storage account xfusionst19335.
1) Copy the contents of xfusion-blob-9948 blob container to the /opt directory on the azure-client host (the landing host once you load this lab).
2) Delete the blob container xfusion-blob-9948 from the storage account.

Solution
Step 1: Copy the blob container contents to /opt directory
First, I need to copy the contents of the xfusion-blob-9948 container to the /opt directory on the azure-client host.

bash
# First, let's check if we're on the azure-client host and the /opt directory exists
ls -la /opt
# Download all blobs from the container
# Correct syntax using -s for source container
az storage blob download-batch \
    --account-name xfusionst19335 \
    --source xfusion-blob-9948 \
    --destination /opt \
    --auth-mode login
# First, get the connection string
az storage account show-connection-string \
    --name xfusionst19335 \
    --resource-group <resource-group-name> \
    --query connectionString

# Then download using connection string
az storage blob download-batch \
    --connection-string "<connection-string>" \
    --source-container xfusion-blob-9948 \
    --destination /opt
Step 2: Delete the blob container
After confirming the files are copied to /opt, delete the container:

bash
# Delete the container
az storage container delete \
    --name xfusion-blob-9948 \
    --account-name xfusionst19335 \
    --auth-mode login

# Or using connection string
az storage container delete \
    --name xfusion-blob-9948 \
    --connection-string "<connection-string>"
